====================================================================================================
PART 5 -- can a field redefinition absorb beta?
====================================================================================================

Candidate redefinitions that could touch a KINETIC coefficient (beta multiplies no
vertex, so only normalisations can move it):
    h_ij -> Omega^2 h_ij ,   N -> chi N ,   P_i -> lam P_i        (Omega, chi, lam const)
Three measured normalisations must be preserved:
    (a) the R3 coefficient  1/(4K)      <- the static law's G           : chi*Omega = 1
    (b) the K_ij K^ij coefficient       <- c_T^2 = 1 (GW170817)         : Omega^3/chi = 1
    (c) the clock coefficient  xi = 1   <- the shape of g_N = g^2/(g+a0): xi*chi*Omega*lam = 1

[P5] log-space system  M.(lnOmega,lnchi,lnlam) = (0, 0, -ln xi)
     det M = 4.00000000000000
C23_DET        lhs=+4.000000000000e+00  rhs=+4.000000000000e+00  rel.res=+0.000e+00  tol=1e-12  PASS   symbolic determinant vs the numerical 3x3 determinant: 4 != 0
     solution: Omega = 1 , chi = 1 , lam = 0.500000000000000
C24_IDENT      lhs=+5.000000000000e-01  rhs=+0.000000000000e+00  rel.res=+5.000e-01  tol=1e-13  FAIL   (Omega,chi,lam) = (1,1,1): the rescaling group is trivial, no direction is left

[P5] a shift redefinition N^i -> N^i + delta N^i changes the reduced action only at
     O(delta^2), because N^i_* is defined by dS/dN^i = 0.  Verified numerically below:
     perturb Y_* = (A/beta) zdot by eps*delta and record the change of the reduced L.

     |Delta L|(eps=1e-3) = 3.422500e-08 ; (eps=5e-4) = 8.556249e-09 ; ratio = 4.000000
     |Delta L|/eps^2 at eps=1e-3 = 3.422500e-02 ; at eps=5e-4 = 3.422500e-02
C26_SHIFT2     lhs=+4.000000415219e+00  rhs=+4.000000000000e+00  rel.res=+1.038e-07  tol=1e-03  PASS   the change is O(eps^2)

[P5] the P normalisation is pinned by the law's SHAPE (xi != 1 deforms g_N)
     xi=1.000 : (g_N - law)/law at g=a0  = +0.000000
     xi=1.010 : (g_N - law)/law at g=a0  = -0.015025
     xi=1.050 : (g_N - law)/law at g=a0  = -0.075610
C27_XI         lhs=-1.666666666667e+00  rhs=+0.000000000000e+00  rel.res=-1.000e+00  tol=1e-12  FAIL   a P rescaling at fixed xi=1 is the identity; at xi=1.05 the law is off by 7.6%

[P5] omega^2 = alpha p^4/Z is invariant under sigma -> lam sigma (both alpha and B scale)
     omega^2 at lam=0.3,1,7 : 1.999999600000e-13 1.999999600000e-13 1.999999600000e-13
C28_SMAT       lhs=+1.000000000000e+00  rhs=+1.000000000000e+00  rel.res=+2.220e-16  tol=1e-14  PASS   omega^2 is rescaling-invariant: no field redefinition can remove Z's beta
     but omega^2(beta=1)/omega^2(beta=3) = 0.733333  -> beta IS an on-shell invariant

